compMin -- takes componentwise minimum or maximum of a list of lists
********************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        compMin L
        compMin(L1,L2)
        compMax L
        compMax(L1,L2)
  * Inputs:
      * L, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, containing lists of the same
        length
  * Outputs:
      * a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, with the same length as those in L

Description
===========

The functions compMin and compMax take the minimum and maximum, respectively,
of a list of lists of integers (or elements of another partially ordered set)
by considering each component separately.

+---------------------------------------+
|i1 : L = {{3,4,1},{5,8,1},{10,2,7}}    |
|                                       |
|o1 = {{3, 4, 1}, {5, 8, 1}, {10, 2, 7}}|
|                                       |
|o1 : List                              |
+---------------------------------------+
|i2 : compMin L                         |
|                                       |
|o2 = {3, 2, 1}                         |
|                                       |
|o2 : List                              |
+---------------------------------------+
|i3 : compMax L                         |
|                                       |
|o3 = {10, 8, 7}                        |
|                                       |
|o3 : List                              |
+---------------------------------------+

The usage compMin(L1,L2) gives the same output as compMin {L1,L2}.

Caveat
======

If L is empty it will be returned unchanged.

Ways to use compMin:
====================

  * "compMin(List)"
  * "compMin(List,List)"

For the programmer
==================

The object *note compMin: compMin, is a *note method function:
(Macaulay2Doc)MethodFunction,.

